      "text": "DOMAIN DRIVEN\nDESIGN",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "containerId": "0rH0zPNf8qQgv8zWZdEuJ",
      "originalText": "DOMAIN DRIVEN DESIGN",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "C5bZaLt28vJuZQVzmG1ob",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -2352.1852579815127,
      "y": -2399.5467921288173,
      "width": 799.9193725585938,
      "height": 1075,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffec99",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b9N",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1252802718,
      "version": 114,
      "versionNonce": 60916866,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1781477279844,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Domain-Driven Design (DDD) - Key Principles\n\nQuick Summary of the 23 Key Principles:\nCore Philosophy (4 Principles)\n\nFocus on the Domain - Business problem is central to design\nUbiquitous Language - Common language between developers and domain experts\nDomain Experts Are Authorities - Business experts define correctness\nSeparation of Concerns - Core domain vs supporting subdomains\n\nStrategic Design (3 Principles)\n\nBounded Contexts - Define explicit boundaries where model is valid\nContext Mapping - Define relationships between contexts\nAggregates - Group related entities with clear boundaries\n\nTactical Patterns (6 Principles)\n\nEntities - Objects defined by identity\nValue Objects - Immutable objects defined by attributes\nDomain Events - Capture significant occurrences\nRepositories - Abstract access to aggregates\nFactories - Encapsulate complex creation\nDomain Services - Stateless operations across aggregates\n\nKey Concepts (3 Principles)\n\nInvariants - Rules that must always be true\nUbiquitous Language Glossary - Document domain terminology\nSubdomain vs Bounded Context - Problem space vs solution space\n\nDesign Practices (4 Principles)\n\nEvent Storming - Collaborative domain discovery\nDesign by Contract - Explicit agreements between components\nLayered Architecture - Organize code by responsibility\nCQRS - Separate read and write models\n\nAnti-Patterns to Avoid (3 Principles)\n\nAnemic Domain Model - Adding behavior, not just data\nGod Objects - Avoid objects doing too much\nIgnoring Business Rules - Enforce in domain model",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Domain-Driven Design (DDD) - Key Principles\n\nQuick Summary of the 23 Key Principles:\nCore Philosophy (4 Principles)\n\nFocus on the Domain - Business problem is central to design\nUbiquitous Language - Common language between developers and domain experts\nDomain Experts Are Authorities - Business experts define correctness\nSeparation of Concerns - Core domain vs supporting subdomains\n\nStrategic Design (3 Principles)\n\nBounded Contexts - Define explicit boundaries where model is valid\nContext Mapping - Define relationships between contexts\nAggregates - Group related entities with clear boundaries\n\nTactical Patterns (6 Principles)\n\nEntities - Objects defined by identity\nValue Objects - Immutable objects defined by attributes\nDomain Events - Capture significant occurrences\nRepositories - Abstract access to aggregates\nFactories - Encapsulate complex creation\nDomain Services - Stateless operations across aggregates\n\nKey Concepts (3 Principles)\n\nInvariants - Rules that must always be true\nUbiquitous Language Glossary - Document domain terminology\nSubdomain vs Bounded Context - Problem space vs solution space\n\nDesign Practices (4 Principles)\n\nEvent Storming - Collaborative domain discovery\nDesign by Contract - Explicit agreements between components\nLayered Architecture - Organize code by responsibility\nCQRS - Separate read and write models\n\nAnti-Patterns to Avoid (3 Principles)\n\nAnemic Domain Model - Adding behavior, not just data\nGod Objects - Avoid objects doing too much\nIgnoring Business Rules - Enforce in domain model",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    }
  ],
